  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 308 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nCentral CONUS upper-level ridging will build toward the U.S. East \nCoast through midweek, while the stalled surface frontal boundary \nover northern Florida gradually washes out. Tuesday will serve as a \ntransition day for South Florida as surface high pressure over the \nwestern Atlantic builds back towards the peninsula, re-establishing \nsouth-southeasterly steering flow. Model guidance remains in good \nagreement that above-normal PWATs will persist across the region, \nsupporting a continued unsettled pattern through Tuesday. While \norganized severe weather is not expected, abundant moisture will \nsupport high rainfall rates and heavy downpours in stronger cells, \nkeeping localized urban and low-lying flooding as the primary \nconcern. \n\nThe western Atlantic high pressure is expected to re-establish full \ncontrol over the region by Wednesday, marking a return to a more \ntypical late-summer easterly flow regime. Drier air filtering into \nthe mid-levels will lower PWATs back toward seasonal norms, helping \nkeep overall convective coverage closer to average through Thursday. \nConvective activity will remain sea-breeze and mesoscale driven, \nwith morning showers and isolated storms pushing across the East \nCoast before focusing inland and over SW Florida through the \nafternoon and early evening.\n\nAnother mid/upper-level trough and associated surface frontal \nboundary will dig south along the Eastern Seaboard by the end of the \nweek, nudging the western Atlantic ridge axis southward once again. \nModel guidance diverges on how far south this boundary will dig and \nthe impact it will have on local weather patterns. However, this \nfeature is expected to lift away quickly heading into the weekend.\n\nHigh temperatures will rise into the lower 90s across most areas, \nwith mid-90s possible across interior SW Florida. Overnight lows \nwill range from the upper 70s/lower 80s along the coasts to the \nlow/mid 70s over the interior. Reduced convective coverage will \nincrease the probability of localized Major HeatRisk across the \nurban corridors during the second half of the week.",
